# fixedbugs, 2026-09-08 (defect 018, found while grounding panel 119's
# llm-ergonomist, which had predicted that the modal first-try failure in
# every arm of its experiment is `a < b` on two `str`).
#
# SYMPTOM: the message that greets exactly that failure named two of the ten
# types it accepts — "`<` takes `i64` or `f64`, found `str`" — so a reader
# holding a `u8` was told the language compares only `i64` and `f64`, and
# converted a value that needed no conversion.
#
# CAUSE: `selfhost/check/ops.hero` accepts `.int_ty | .float_ty`, which is all
# eight integer widths and both floats, and then handed the literal string
# "`i64` or `f64`" to `value_errors.bad_operand` at three sites: the
# comparison, a `str` under an arithmetic operator, and unary `-`. The
# checker was right and its own text was wrong.
#
# FIX: "any integer or a float", which is the vocabulary `print`'s own message
# already used ("`print` takes any integer, a float, `bool` or `str`"). The
# sibling `mixed_arithmetic` had been saying the true thing the whole time —
# "two integers of the SAME width, or two floats of the same width" — and it
# fires whenever the two sides DIFFER, which is why the wrong text was only
# ever reached by two sides of one non-ordered type: the shape a reader writes
# first.
#
# This case pins all three sites and both of the shapes that reach the first.

function main()
    a = "apple"
    b = "banana"
    print(a < b)  #~ bad_operand
    yes = true
    no = false
    print(yes < no)  #~ bad_operand
    print(-a)  #~ bad_operand
    print("x" * "y")  #~ bad_operand
